    {
      "id": "surface-meaning",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Meaning",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": -640
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Meaning",
        "body": "**What is this thing?**\n\nA Surface is a named, independently-resolved presentation track. It lets one node or graph offer more than one complete way of being shown — each scoped to a different hosting context — instead of a single universal ladder every host is expected to interpret the same way.\n\nThe motivating case: a Godzilla node is perfectly ordinary on the graph canvas — glyph, icon, summary, detail, the conventional ladder. But the same node placed on a map needs a completely different presentation: a map doesn't want a \"detail card,\" it wants something that reads as a map marker at every zoom level a map actually has. Those aren't two points on one continuum. They're two different continuums, because the thing being measured — screen occupancy in a card layout vs. apparent size on a map — is different. A Surface is the name for one of those continuums. A node with map ambitions declares a second Surface; a node without them just has the one every graph already has by default.\n\nA Surface does not render anything itself. It is a **chain of faces**, richest first, each claiming its own point on that Surface's resolution scale, **ending in a Glyph** so that every Surface carries a mark of its own. A host resolves against one Surface's chain.\n\nA Surface is how a graph is seen from outside. Each of its faces is how the graph looks at one size, for someone who has not entered it: on another graph’s ground, in a Portal, or as a tile in the workspace. What a visitor finds inside is a different thing: the ground, which is where they arrive and what they stand on.\n\nSurface has semantic identity as a named presentation track. Its encoding as an edge plus a chain does not make it less fundamental than a node-represented concept; the index records meaning and representation separately.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "definitionKey": "page-section",
        "_classBinding": {
