    {
      "id": "surface-behavior",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Behavior",
      "position": {
        "x": -180,
        "y": 80
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Behavior",
        "body": "**What does it participate in — and what's real today versus not built?**\n\n- `collectSurfaceChains()` (`utils/surfaceChains.js`) reads every Surface of a graph. Malformed chains resolve the same way every time and never throw: a cycle stops at the first repeated node, a branch follows the first outgoing edge, a Glyph ends the walk, a head that is not a face is skipped, a duplicate id keeps the first, a missing default falls to the first Surface. Each is reported as a warning.\n- The resolution ladder (`resolvePercentageGraphView()`) is built from the chosen Surface's own chain, and below every rung the Surface shows its own Glyph. The resolution math (`viewResolution.js`) is unchanged.\n- One resolver decides the glyph (`resolveSurfaceGlyph`): the Glyph that ends the chain, then the root's own glyph.\n- `resolveSurface(graph, surfaceId, { hosts })`: an explicit request wins; the default answers otherwise; a Surface the host cannot render is never chosen.\n- New graphs are created with one Surface, the editor draws and lists Surfaces (drag from the `surface` handle to add one; drag from a face's `sequence-out` handle to extend a chain), and the validator checks each chain.\n\n**Not built:**\n- The workspace and camera code still ask for the default Surface; nothing passes a Surface or a host yet.\n- A 3D face. No face has a `node.3d.*` payload and nothing renders one.\n- A host declaring a context that picks the default Surface.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "definitionKey": "page-section",
