    {
      "id": "surface-representations",
      "type": "page-section",
      "label": "Representations",
      "position": {
        "x": 380,
        "y": 80
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "data": {
        "title": "Representations",
        "body": "**How is it represented?**\n\nNever directly. A Surface itself has no visual form — what renders is always whichever face its rendering ladder resolves to, for the host and resolution asking. Two different hosts looking at the same node \"at the same moment\" may be showing faces from two entirely different Surfaces, each independently correct for its own context — a map viewport and the graph canvas are not disagreeing with each other; they were never asking the same question.\n\nSurface selection and resolution do not implicitly resize the workspace frame or reflow neighboring graphs. Focusing a graph draws it at its own authored size, whatever size it was dragged to in the workspace while it was not focused. Manual resizing is explicit: on a focused graph it sets the graph’s own window, on an unfocused one only its size in this workspace. Zoom scales the workspace rather than changing authored dimensions. Internal nodes, edges, handles and pointer targets are clipped to the frame; exposed boundary controls stay accessible. A face keeps the workspace title bar; the terminal Glyph does not acquire one.\n\nA face can show glyph-shaped artwork without being named Icon; it remains distinct from the chain’s terminal Glyph.",
        "presentation": {
          "glyph": {
            "kind": "icon",
            "name": "Article"
          },
          "baseLevel": "detail"
        },
        "definitionKey": "page-section",
        "_classBinding": {
